WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.800
Vision is a classic Marvel character, now
known to millions as part of the MCU.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.580
From his humble roots in the comics to his
unique story on screen, this is everything

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.020
you don't know about the mysterious android.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.849
Vision may be the most popular character with
that name in the Marvel universe, but he wasn't

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.390
the first to claim the moniker.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:21.390
Decades before the Vision you know first appeared,
a totally different character named Vision

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.960
appeared in 1940's Marvel Mystery Comics #13.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.019
Also operating under the name Aarkus, this
Vision is an alien who has teleportation and

00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:30.289
ice-based superpowers.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:34.440
He occasionally fights alongside superheroes
like the Human Torch and Namor as part of

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.800
the Invaders, an early Marvel superhero team.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.179
In the 1960s, a number of these classic Golden
Age characters, including Captain America

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:43.239
and Namor, came back into the spotlight.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:46.579
Vision, however, was not lucky enough to receive
this treatment.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.949
Though comics writer Roy Writer initially
wanted to revive Aarkus for the 1960s, company

00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:55.079
mandates forced him to create a new character
for the name Vision to be applied to instead.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:59.449
Thus, the very first Vision in Marvel Comics
history quickly faded into obscurity.

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:03.050
However, that doesn't mean this early Vision
has been totally forgotten.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.510
Since the 1970s, Aarkus has managed to make
a handful of appearances in the comics, including

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.330
a 2013 X-Men: Legacy storyline.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.910
Most recently, he managed to become a playable
character in the mobile video game Marvel

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.170
Contest of Champions.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.280
Some superheroes like to dress exclusively
in bleak colors.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.680
Not so for Vision: This particular Avenger
is readily identifiable by his head-to-toe

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.720
bold colors.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.240
A yellow cape and gloves compliment Vision's
green body and red face — he's easy to spot

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.280
in any crowd.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.690
However, his distinct look wasn't popular
with one very important person at Marvel.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.250
Creator Roy Thomas explained to The Hollywood
Reporter,

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.310
"...Stan [Lee] hated the color of The Vision.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.540
'Why'd you make him red?

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.800
Red's not a good color!'

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.040
I didn't want to make him green like The Hulk
or Blue like the Atlanteans.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.641
I suppose I could have made him white, but
the paper we printed on was so poor, that

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.650
you would have been able to see the other
side, so we didn't make things white that

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.670
we didn't have to.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.690
Though Lee wasn't initially satisfied with
Vision's colors, it's those same hues that

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.670
have made him one of the most visually distinctive
Marvel superheroes around.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:05.020
Sometimes, iconic characters come about due
to spur-of-the-moment creative inspiration.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.820
Others, like Vision, come out of something
as simple as a mandate from the boss.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.810
According to Roy Thomas, Vision was created
as a solution to Stan Lee's mandate for new

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.170
Avengers members.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.440
Thomas explained to Yahoo,

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.990
"Stan suddenly said it was time to make up
a new character, to bring in a new Avenger.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.470
I didn't like to create new characters for
Marvel, because I wouldn't own them.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.570
I wanted to bring back the old Vision ... But
Stan wanted an android.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:26.780
He didn't say why.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.390
He just said he wanted the new character to
be an android."

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.480
Thus, the Vision the world knows and loves
was born, all thanks to Stan Lee's insistence

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.670
on an android Avenger.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.330
It may not be the glossiest origin story for
an iconic superhero, but Lee's mandate did

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.690
have its advantages.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:45.180
Chiefly, it immediately helped make Vision
stand out on any Avengers roster — and still

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.180
does, in fact.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.100
The distinguished superhero team has had all
kinds of unusual members, but an android like

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.120
Vision still manages to catch your attention
all these years later.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.280
In the 50-plus years he's existed, Vision
has often drawn comparisons to Star Trek's

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.280
Spock.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.280
It isn't hard to see why.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.810
On the surface, there's the fact that they're
both sci-fi characters introduced in the late

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:04.810
1960s.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.890
On a deeper, more specific level, they're
both no-nonsense figures who are usually the

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.330
only non-humanoid members of their group.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.230
What does Thomas say about these comparisons?

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.230
He told Yahoo,

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.700
"People have told me they see a resemblance
to Mr. Spock.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.370
If there is, I didn't do it consciously.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.180
I was vaguely aware of the show.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.960
We would play poker while our wives and kids
sat in the next room watching Star Trek.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.470
So I would hear the TV playing."

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.709
Rather than using Spock as a model, Thomas
actually drew inspiration from a robot named

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.770
Adam Link in Otto Binder's short story, "I,
Robot."

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:37.540
In this tale, which also inspired Isaac Asimov's
book of the same name, Link is falsely accused

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.030
of murdering his creator.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.170
In the ensuring chaos, Link becomes all too
aware of the adverse effects his very existence

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.760
has on human beings, who are immediately suspicious
of a robot.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.080
Adam Link's tragically self-conscious qualities
are immediately apparent in Vision, in addition

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.890
to the character's unintentional overlap with
Mr. Spock.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.890
connection

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.319
In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Vision is
played by actor Paul Bettany.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:02.590
Prior to his multi-film turn as Vision, Bettany
appeared in a wide variety of movies.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.790
Among those acting credits was an earlier
role in the MCU as Tony Stark's A.I. helper,

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:07.790
J.A.R.V.I.S.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.790
"Jarvis, you there?"

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:09.790
"At your service, sir."

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:13.849
A reimagining of the comics' Edwin Jarvis,
who is a human butler, J.A.R.V.I.S. was not

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:16.099
a role that Bettany initially put much stock
in.

00:04:16.099 --> 00:04:19.989
At the time of Iron Man's release, Bettany
said that while he was recording his voicework

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:23.210
for J.A.R.V.I.S., he didn't actually know
that it was for a superhero movie.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.780
While Bettany started out as an outsider oblivious
to the larger machinations of the Marvel Cinematic

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.669
Universe, he didn't remain that way for long.

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:31.669
After playing J.A.R.V.I.S.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:36.169
across four different MCU installments, it
was announced in February 2014 that Bettany

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:38.990
would be taking on a physical role in the
MCU as Vision.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.770
When Joss Whedon was putting together his
ideas for Avengers: Age of Ultron, he intended

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.050
to use the mammoth box office success of the
original Avengers to realize some of his grander

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:49.050
ambitions.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.380
Specifically, he wanted to use Ultron as the
primary villain and introduce Vision, as played

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:54.629
by Paul Bettany.

00:04:54.629 --> 00:04:58.270
Like many of Whedon's plans for Age of Ultron,
this was a notion he had carried with him

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:01.520
long before the cameras even started rolling
on the first Avengers movie.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:02.520
He told Collider,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.819
"Before I took the first [Avengers], I said,
'Well, I don't know if I'm right for this

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:09.900
or if I want it or you want me, but in the
second one, the villain has [to be] Ultron

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.199
and he has to create the Vision, and then,
that has to be Paul Bettany.'"

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:17.439
Whedon went on to note that his fascination
with the character derives from Vision's unusual

00:05:17.439 --> 00:05:21.509
power set, as well as the fact that Vision
was a focal point of the comics he grew up

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:22.509
reading.

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:26.039
As for his longstanding dream of having Bettany
inhabit the role, the director joked that

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:28.770
Bettany's sharp cheekbones make him a natural
fit for the part.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:33.539
Though he's existed for over half a century
in the Marvel Comics canon, Vision has usually

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:35.309
acted as a supporting player.

00:05:35.309 --> 00:05:39.290
First introduced in the comics as a creation
of Ultron who is convinced to fight back against

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:43.189
his creator, Vision subsequently became a
fixture of the Avengers comic books.

00:05:43.189 --> 00:05:47.370
Over the years, Vision has factored prominently
into the Avengers' adventures — the wedding

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.569
between himself and Scarlet Witch even acts
as the centerpiece of Giant-Size Avengers

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:52.569
#4.

00:05:52.569 --> 00:05:56.219
Still, being a standout member of an ensemble
is not the same thing as headlining your own

00:05:56.219 --> 00:06:00.839
comic series, a feat that most other Marvel
superheroes have been able to do regularly.

00:06:00.839 --> 00:06:03.909
While Vision has been a supporting player
for most of his fictional life, he's still

00:06:03.909 --> 00:06:07.559
played a major role in numerous storylines
that have shaped the Marvel universe.

00:06:07.559 --> 00:06:11.789
Moreover, the character has managed to secure
a handful of solo adventures over the years.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:16.279
The first of these was a four-issue Vision
series published in 1994 by Bob Harras and

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:17.369
Manny Clark.

00:06:17.369 --> 00:06:22.879
More recent is 2015's 12-issue Vision series
by Tom King and Gabriel Hernandez Walta.

00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:27.249
Even if Vision isn't a go-to figure for headlining
Marvel comic books, these acclaimed stories

00:06:27.249 --> 00:06:30.759
starring the mysterious character show the
value Vision has as a leading man.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.259
If you're going to be a superhero in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, you're going to have to

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:36.520
put on an elaborate costume.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.939
Sometimes, this costume is the motion-capture
suit Mark Ruffalo has to wear to portray the

00:06:40.939 --> 00:06:41.939
Hulk.

00:06:41.939 --> 00:06:45.369
Other times, it's the patriotic get-up Chris
Evans had to pull on to Captain America.

00:06:45.369 --> 00:06:49.909
In the case of Paul Bettany, properly portraying
Vision means having to undergo extensive application

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:51.529
of makeup and prosthetics.

00:06:51.529 --> 00:06:56.530
Transforming Bettany into the boldly-colored
Vision is not an easy task: A two-hour process

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.930
is required to make the man into the machine.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:03.309
Perfecting Vision's look proved to be a time-consuming
task even in the concept stage, well before

00:07:03.309 --> 00:07:05.610
Paul Bettany took his seat in the makeup chair.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:09.089
The makeup artists for Avengers: Age of Ultron
were so determined to get the character's

00:07:09.089 --> 00:07:13.069
unique look right that they put extraordinary
effort into mocking up some prototypes for

00:07:13.069 --> 00:07:14.430
the final product.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.979
Makeup artist Nik Williams told Make-up Artist
magazine in 2015,

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:20.159
"The make-up went through a lot of stages.

00:07:20.159 --> 00:07:24.121
In order to make the lines absolutely perfect,
as though he was born from a computer, we

00:07:24.121 --> 00:07:28.270
actually 'sculpted' the make-up in 3-D over
a cyber-scan of Paul's head and printed out

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.430
in clear plastic."

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:34.099
While Vision's look wasn't easy to put together,
all this exacting effort certainly paid off.

00:07:34.099 --> 00:07:37.470
Vision came to the silver screen looking like
he'd walked straight out of a comic book.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.589
Vision isn't the first character to carry
that moniker in the comics, so it should be

00:07:41.589 --> 00:07:45.139
no surprise that he's also not the last character
to bear the name either.

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:50.189
Whereas the Vision created in 1967 is a massive
departure from the one created in the 1940s,

00:07:50.189 --> 00:07:54.259
though, the newest superheroes to carry on
the Vision name have been directly inspired

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:56.159
by the crimson-colored android.

00:07:56.159 --> 00:07:59.759
In 2005, a new version of Vision debuted in
Young Avengers #5.

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:03.819
While he looks like a dead-ringer for the
original Vision, this new hero is younger

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:05.499
and goes by the name Jonas.

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:09.029
Speaking of newer and younger versions of
Vision, the mainstream Vision's most notable

00:08:09.029 --> 00:08:12.960
superhero successor emerged in the form of
his own daughter, Viv Vision.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.930
First appearing in Vision #1, the character
starts out as one part of Vision's fragmented

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.919
attempt to create a traditional American nuclear
family for himself.

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:24.889
In the years since that run, Viv has blossomed
into a standalone superhero in her own right.

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:29.550
In a 2018 interview, Bettany revealed that
the chance to portray Vision in the Marvel

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.380
Cinematic Universe came just as it looked
like his acting opportunities were about to

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.380
dry up.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.639
"I had just had a meeting with a producer,
who will remain nameless, who told me that

00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:42.319
my career was over."

00:08:42.319 --> 00:08:45.759
For a short time after that meeting, the actor
was gripped with despair, before getting a

00:08:45.759 --> 00:08:48.360
phone call from Age of Ultron director Joss
Whedon.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:52.160
Whedon proceeded to ask if Bettany wanted
to play Vision in the then-upcoming Avengers

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.160
sequel.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.699
That one phone call turned things around for
Bettany completely.

00:08:55.699 --> 00:08:57.160
As Bettany reflected to Insider,

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.880
"I sort of looked up to heaven and thought
karma's very quick these days ... It happened

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:01.880
just like that.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.470
I sat down and my phone rang.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:04.470
Very funny.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.990
But you know if I had a dollar for every time
somebody told me my career was over, my career

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:08.990
could be over."

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:12.949
Since then, Bettany has portrayed Vision in
a number of MCU titles, and the franchise's

00:09:12.949 --> 00:09:14.899
first Disney+ series, WandaVision.

00:09:14.899 --> 00:09:19.380
Beyond the MCU, Bettany has also landed roles
in films like Solo: A Star Wars Story and

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:20.399
Uncle Frank.

00:09:20.399 --> 00:09:24.180
So whoever it was who said Bettany's career
was over just moments before he landed one

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.459
of the biggest roles of his career probably
needs to have their own vision checked.

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:30.790
